---

<Layout title="About | Farida Sakr" description={description}>
	<article>
		<h1>About</h1>

		<p>
			I'm a data scientist. The part of the work I care about is the part that happens before any
			model is fit: deciding what would have to be true for a number to mean what someone is about
			to say it means. Choosing between gradient boosting and a regression is usually a smaller
			decision than choosing what the comparison group is, and only one of those two decisions
			shows up in the accuracy metric.
		</p>

		<p>
			That's why the identification question interests me more than the modelling question. A
			promotion analysis that measures the promotion plus the fact that the best customers took it
			isn't a modelling failure; it's an answer to a question nobody asked, delivered with
			confidence intervals. The failure mode I keep running into isn't a wrong estimate, it's a
			precise one about the wrong estimand.
		</p>

		<p>
			Both projects here are built on synthetic data with published ground truth, and that's
			deliberate rather than a shortcut around finding real data. If the true effect is known, an
			estimate can be scored: this method missed by 9%, that one by 300%. On real data the same
			disagreement becomes a meeting. It's the only setup I've found where an analysis, or an
			agent's conclusion, can be marked right or wrong instead of argued about, which is also why
			the honest limitations are on the case study pages rather than in a footnote.
		</p>

		<p>
			<strong>What I'm looking for:</strong> senior data scientist roles with real ownership of the
			work, where I carry a problem from framing the question through to the decision it changes. Most
			of my experience is B2B, working with both internal and external stakeholders, and it has shaped
			how I think about impact: an estimate only matters once the person acting on it understands what
			it does and doesn't support.
		</p>

